> Am I able to specify that a portion of my estate should be distributed by someone (or a foundation created to do it), rather than specify certain organizations that the money should go to? Is it worth it? I'm talking about 10% of my money, which is currently about $60k, but let's say it might get up to double that, by the time I retire.
**assistant**:
> Yes. Rather than naming charities now, you can generally direct that a portion of your estate be used for charitable purposes selected later by a person or institution you appoint.
> 
> The usual ways to do this are:
> 
> 1. **Give your executor discretion in your will**  
>    For example: “I give 10% of my residuary estate to one or more IRS-qualified public charities selected by my executor, with preference for organizations advancing climate mitigation, global health, and effective poverty reduction.”
> 
> 2. **Create a charitable trust under your will**  
>    A *testamentary charitable trust* can appoint a trustee to distribute the funds under criteria you set. This gives more formal structure and can continue over time, but has legal and administrative costs.
> 
> 3. **Leave the money to a community foundation or donor-advised fund sponsor**  
>    You can ask it to establish a small field-of-interest fund, or direct its board to make grants within a defined purpose. This is often the most administratively sensible institutional option if you want continuity beyond one trusted individual.
> 
> 4. **Give a trusted person a defined power to recommend or select charities**  
>    This can be structured so that a trustee or foundation is legally responsible for distributing the funds, while your chosen person advises or selects recipients within stated limits.
> 
> ## Is it worth doing for $6,000–$12,000?
> 
> Probably **not as a standalone private foundation or long-lived charitable trust**.
> 
> A private foundation is particularly ill-suited here: it requires ongoing administration, tax filings (including Form 990-PF), investment and payout rules, and accounting. Those burdens can consume a meaningful share of a $6k–$12k gift. A formal trust can likewise be disproportionate unless it is part of a larger estate plan or has some special family-governance purpose.
> 
> But it can absolutely be worth preserving **flexibility**. Organizations change, dissolve, get captured by bad incentives, or simply cease to be the best use of money over several decades. If your aim is for the money to go to the *best opportunities at the time of your death*, rather than to today’s favored institutions, a discretionary charitable bequest is sensible.
> 
> For this amount, I would usually favor a simple clause like:
> 
> > I give 10% of my residuary estate to such one or more organizations described in Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code, and qualifying as public charities, as my Executor shall select in the Executor’s reasonable discretion. In making those selections, my Executor should prioritize evidence-based organizations that advance human flourishing and global sustainability, including, where appropriate, climate change mitigation and adaptation, reduction of extreme poverty, global health, and effective institutional capacity. No distribution shall be made to a private foundation, political campaign, or organization substantially engaged in lobbying.
> 
> You could also name a specific trusted person as the selector, with your executor carrying out that decision:
> 
> > My Executor shall distribute this bequest among qualified public charities as recommended in writing by [Name]. If [Name] is unable or unwilling to serve, my Executor shall make the selections under the criteria stated above.
> 
> That keeps costs low, avoids locking in specific charities, and gives someone who knows your values the ability to adapt.
> 
> ## Practical cautions
> 
> - **Be specific enough to be enforceable.** “Give it to good causes” is weak. A purpose area, eligible-recipient definition, priorities, and exclusions make the instruction more reliable.
> - **Name backups.** Your chosen person may predecease you, decline, or be incapacitated.
> - **Avoid placing a burden on your executor without compensation or clarity.** Selecting and vetting charities takes time; consider whether your executor is genuinely the right person.
> - **Use a fallback.** For example, if no selection is made within 12 months, the executor can distribute it to a named community foundation’s general charitable fund or to a set of named fallback charities.
> - **Keep a nonbinding letter of wishes.** Your will can contain the durable legal rules; a separate letter can explain your philosophy, favored causes, examples of organizations, and how you would assess effectiveness. It is easy to update without re-executing your will.
> - **Get state-specific drafting advice.** The enforceability and mechanics depend on your state’s trust and probate law. An estates-and-trusts lawyer can turn this into a short, durable provision.
> 
> At your projected scale, my opinion is: **make the 10% bequest flexible, but keep its administration one-time and simple.** Use either a trusted selector plus a clear charitable-purpose clause, or a community foundation if you prefer institutional continuity. Do not create a private foundation solely for this bequest.
