> Thanks. What about guardian for my children? Should I choose my parents-- who I would love to act in this role, but won't be able to in 20 years-- or someone else (I don't know who I'd choose).
**assistant**:
> You do not need to identify the theoretically perfect person for every possible future date. You need to make the best **current, revisable** choice—and then revisit it periodically.
> 
> My instinct, given what you said, is:
> 
> - **Name your parents as first-choice guardians now** *if* they are willing, healthy, have a strong relationship with the boys, and would provide the kind of home and values you want in the event something happened soon.
> - **Also name at least one, preferably two, younger alternate guardians.**
> - Revisit the choice every few years, or after any major change: a move, a health change, divorce, new close friendships, children reaching school milestones, etc.
> 
> If you die 15–20 years from now, your parents may be too old to take on parenting—and by then your children may also be near adulthood. The document can simply provide that your parents serve only if willing and able; otherwise the alternate serves. You do not need to predict the exact future now.
> 
> ## A useful way to frame the decision
> 
> Ask two separate questions:
> 
> 1. **If both parents died this year, who would give the boys the most loving, stable immediate home?**
> 2. **If that person could not do it—or if the death occurred much later—who would be the best durable parent figure?**
> 
> Your parents may be the clear answer to the first question even if they are not the answer to the second. That is a completely reasonable estate-plan design.
> 
> You could make the sequence something like:
> 
> 1. Your parents, jointly or the surviving/able one;
> 2. A younger sibling, cousin, or close friend/couple;
> 3. Another alternate;
> 4. If none can serve, a court selects a guardian based on the children’s best interests.
> 
> The law generally does not force an unwilling or incapable nominee to take the role. A court ultimately retains authority to act in the children’s best interests, but a thoughtful parental nomination carries substantial weight.
> 
> ## It is okay not to know the alternate yet
> 
> Many parents find this harder than writing the financial provisions. The standard is not “Who is exactly like us?”—there is no such person. A better test is:
> 
> - Do they genuinely love the children and want them?
> - Are they emotionally stable, responsible, and kind?
> - Would the children feel safe and wanted in their home?
> - Do they share enough of your core values—education, curiosity, health, ethics, openness, community—even if their day-to-day parenting differs?
> - Could they keep the brothers together?
> - Would they support continued relationships with both sides of the family?
> - Is their partnership/home situation likely to be reasonably stable?
> - Would they actually say yes after having an honest conversation?
> 
> A person need not be your closest friend, wealthiest relative, or your parenting twin. Love, steadiness, sound judgment, and willingness matter much more.
> 
> ## Separate the caregiving role from the money role
> 
> You can name:
> 
> - a **guardian of the children**: raises them; and
> - a **trustee**: manages their inheritance, pays for their needs, and releases money over time.
> 
> Those can be the same person, but they need not be. In fact, separating them can be wise: it avoids placing both the emotional labor of raising children and the fiduciary burden of managing assets on one household. It can also reduce any appearance that the guardian benefits financially from money intended for the boys—though the trust should absolutely allow the trustee to pay reasonable expenses of raising them.
> 
> You can direct that the trustee work collaboratively with the guardian and fund housing, schooling, activities, therapy, travel to see family, and other things that enable a good childhood.
> 
> ## Include a nonbinding “letter of wishes”
> 
> A will should name the guardians and alternates plainly. Then separately, write a letter—easy to update—covering matters such as:
> 
> - keeping the brothers together;
> - maintaining meaningful connections with grandparents, aunts/uncles, and family friends;
> - educational and religious/cultural preferences;
> - your hopes for their identity, curiosity, health, friendships, and opportunities;
> - whether you would prefer that they remain in their current school/community where practical;
> - your broad approach to money and adulthood.
> 
> This should be guidance, not an attempt to control their entire childhood from beyond the grave. The guardian will need room to respond to real circumstances.
> 
> ## A practical next step
> 
> I would not leave the document without any nomination while searching for perfection. Name your parents as first choice if that is your honest present judgment, and begin deliberately considering alternates over the next few months. You and your wife should discuss a short list, then have direct conversations with the candidates before naming them.
> 
> And revise the plan on a regular cadence—say every 3–5 years. Guardianship nominations are among the most normal parts of an estate plan to update as children, relationships, locations, and health evolve.
